Q: How to check at compile-time if a = b (where a and b are arguments of type T&amp;) is valid inside a template function? A: <blockquote>
<p>I’m looking for a method that <strong>doesn’t require actually performing the assignment</strong>, ideally a trait or concept-based solution. <em>Is there a way in standard c++ to detect at compile-time whether a = b will compile</em> for a given type T inside a function template?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, with C++20, you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/requires.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>requires</code></a> to check the validity of the expression <code>x= y</code> without actually performing the assignment as you require(pun intended) as shown below. Note also that I've included some separate checks like checking if the elements of the vector are copy constructible etc or not.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;class T&gt; 
void foo(T&amp; a, const T&amp; b) {
                                         //element's copy assignable or not check here
     constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE = std::is_copy_assignable_v&lt;T&gt; &amp;&amp;
                                         requires
                                         {
                                            a=b ;  //checking if the assignment is valid or not 
                                         } &amp;&amp;
    //element's copy construble or not check here
    std::is_copy_constructible_v&lt;typename T::value_type&gt;;

    if constexpr (IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE)
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;assignable&quot;&lt;&lt; std::endl;
    else
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;not assignable&quot;&lt;&lt; std::endl;   

}


int main() {
    using X = std::vector&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt;;
    X v1, v2;
    foo&lt;X&gt;(v1, v2); // prints not assignable
   using Y = std::vector&lt;int&gt;;
   Y v3, v4;
    foo&lt;Y&gt;(v3,v4);   //prints assignable
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bs6oWz1PY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Working demo</a></p>
<hr />
<p>It is <strong>trivial</strong> to modify this method to use <code>concept</code> and is left as an exercise for the OP.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Important Note</h3>
<p>Note that one may think that we can define <code>IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE</code> like:</p>
<pre><code>constexpr bool IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE =   requires { a=b; };
</code></pre>
<p>but this won't work for <code>std::vector</code> because of a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/18405291/12002570">design flaw in <code>std::vector</code></a>. This is why I've used <code>std::is_copy_assignable_v</code> and <code>std::is_copy_constructible_v</code> in addition to checking <code>a=b</code> in the definition of <code>IS_COPY_ASSIGNABLE</code>.</p>

Q: ERROR undefined, HttpErrorResponse&#160;{headers: HttpHeaders, status: 404, statusText: &quot;Not Found&quot; A: <p>Your variable <code>mtr</code> is indeed undefined. </p>

<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>mtr: '45';

console.log(mtr);
// undefined
</code></pre>

<p>You need to set the variable to the '45' string instead you typed the <code>mtr</code>variable as type '45'.</p>

<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>mtr: string = '45';

console.log(mtr);
// '45'
</code></pre>
